hw_ppu_frame.inc: timestamped native background input service
Reference: hw_ppu.c bg_fetch/bg_commit, general_dot/general_half_dot,
compute_pixel/output_pixel and the reference register/data-port adapters.

The FRAME provider owns admitted background-input packets, retains actual
nametable/attribute/plane reads and commits, and publishes the existing
three-dot color pipeline. A later bank/register write cannot reconstruct or
replace an input already fetched. Ordinary object selection and sprite-row
composition remain scanline operations; the sprite presentation uses primary
OAM and the selected native row. Unsupported boards/regions retain the
build's reference provider.

PAL (2C07) on fixed-PRG NROM/CNROM uses the same provider. Its pre-render
line is hw_prerender_line() (311), not 261, and the red/green emphasis bits
are swapped exactly as output_pixel does. A PAL native interval starts at
tick 1 and stays within the current scanline: N CPU cycles produce
floor((phase+16N)/5) dots, with half-dots two master ticks after each dot.
That keeps the NTSC sample/half order, so eight-dot tile jobs and partial
packets are reused unchanged. PAL MMC1/MMC3 and the reference providers keep
refusing PAL intervals.

Current-build states retain the actual bounded background pipeline and the
native sprite row. FRAME schema 7 deliberately replaces the prior disposable
prefix representation. Other PPU providers retain their layout and adapter.
The implementation is portable C and adds no live backend/profile switch.

Stable admitted intervals use the existing raster row decoder and output
kernel as one eight-pixel input job: four source reads, one history commit
and one output span. The real phase service owns partial inputs, pending
writes, bus-state settling, recording consumers and raster boundaries. The
job never reads beyond an interval which excludes source mutations and
interrupt observations.

HD recording consumes the native sprite row's owned tile, attributes and
screen origin. Those inputs remain in the existing serialized HD tile record;
the picture observer does not depend on reference sprite-shifter progress.
The reference HD observer keeps its original interface and pixel positions.
The synthetic HD input probe clocks real fetch phases before a bank change.

Validation completed for this source: the FRAME/APU-operation SMB3 cold route
matches HLE for all 2,400 recorded frame products, PCM, memory, screenshots
and periodic presentation products. Current-build continuation is exact.
The FRAME/APU-operation state test passes 28 ordinary and four pending-input
snapshot cases, plus 28 bulk-packet versus individual-timestamp comparisons,
across every CPU/PPU alignment. Further game, performance, platform and
netplay qualification remains separate from these checks.
The synthetic HD pipeline gate passes CHR ROM/RAM, bank changes, scroll,
sprite flips and 8/16-pixel rows, four alignments, and full-state replay.
The fetched-input probe uses complete states, which include the HD observer;
the unclocked guest-call snapshot deliberately excludes presentation state.
